1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,110
Your client asks for a change, in chat.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:08,270
Usually this is where the form, the queue and the wait begin.

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:12,260
Tally reads the thread instead. The message is the request.

4
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:18,070
It finds the client in your CRM and recalls their deal and your price list.

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:22,370
The part that makes this safe: the model reads and asks.

6
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:26,390
Code decides and pays, inside a written policy.

7
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:30,030
Code checks the clauses and creates the Stripe invoice:

8
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:34,320
five thousand pounds, yearly, due in seven days.

9
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:40,210
The client gets the reply and the PDF in the same chat. They never left it.

10
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:47,950
Every pound carries a receipt: their words, the remembered fact, the clause.

11
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:55,050
Your team gets a note, not a ticket. The books stay right.

12
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:00,830
Now change one thing. A dispute crosses a written limit.

13
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:04,340
Code stops and asks a human, in the same chat.

14
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:07,130
One word pauses everything.

15
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:09,760
Said. Done. Billed.

16
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:14,360
The conversation is the contract. The agent is the back office.
